{"entries":{"chapter-97-study-aging-of-the-endocrine-system-and-non-thyroid-endocrine-disorders|Chapter 97 Study: Aging of the Endocrine System and Non-Thyroid Endocrine Disorders":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"general-principles|General Principles":["u3"],"copy|Most clinical studies define endocrine dysfunction against young-adult reference ranges, not age-specific ones; hypogonadism in older men is defined by testosterone below young-male norms, while the upper limit of normal serum thyrotropin rises with age (~7 to 8 mIU/L in those 80+) (p1526).":["u4"],"copy|Hormone levels can be transiently altered by acute illness (eg, slight TSH elevation in recovery) or drugs (eg, opioids suppressing gonadotropin/testosterone) — repeat testing once the patient's baseline health is restored before acting on an abnormal result (p1526).":["u5"],"copy|Hormone replacement doses in older adults may be lower than young-adult doses because metabolic clearance of thyroid hormone, hydrocortisone, and testosterone falls with age (p1526).":["u6"],"pituitary|Pituitary":["u7"],"copy|Hyperprolactinemia tiers: mild 20–50 ng/mL (rarely needs treatment, just exclude primary hypothyroidism with TSH), moderate 50–250 ng/mL (refer — likely prolactinoma), severe >250 ng/mL (refer — almost always a large prolactinoma) (p1525, p1528).":["u8"],"copy|Pituitary incidentaloma prevalence 10–20% on autopsy/imaging series; macroincidentaloma defined as ≥1 cm and needs a fuller hormone-deficiency workup plus serial MRI every 1–2 years (p1528–1529).":["u9"],"copy|Chronic hypopituitarism incidence <0.5 per 100,000/yr; most common deficits are secondary hypogonadism and hypothyroidism, but secondary hypoadrenalism, though less common, can be life-threatening — these patients need an alert bracelet (p1529).":["u10"],"adrenal|Adrenal":["u11"],"copy|Cushing syndrome: the most common cause in older patients is iatrogenic (supraphysiologic corticosteroid dosing) (p1526, p1530).":["u12"],"copy|Adrenal insufficiency: the most common cause is rapid discontinuation of chronic glucocorticoid therapy (p1526, p1531).":["u13"],"copy|Adrenal insufficiency sick-day rule: triple the daily hydrocortisone dose (~60 mg) for 3 days with fever ≥101.5°F; give 100 mg IM hydrocortisone if oral intake is impossible (p1526, p1531–1532).":["u14"],"copy|Usual glucocorticoid replacement: 15–20 mg hydrocortisone/day in divided doses, avoiding late-afternoon/evening dosing (insomnia risk) (p1531).":["u15"],"copy|The standard cosyntropin test measures peak serum cortisol at 30 or 60 minutes; the traditional, assay-dependent threshold is 18 μg/dL (500 nmol/L), and p1531 misprints the unit as mg/dL (Endocrine Society, recommendation 2.1).Current practice (not the book): peak cortisol below 500 nmol/L (18 μg/dL, assay dependent) at 30 or 60 min indicates adrenal insufficiency — Endocrine Society primary adrenal insufficiency guideline, 2016.":["u16"],"copy|The chapter reports incidental adrenal masses in ~4% of high-resolution abdominal CT scans and greater malignancy risk above 4 cm (p1532); a homogeneous mass measuring ≤10 HU on unenhanced CT has benign imaging features and needs no further imaging under ESE/ENSAT recommendation 2.3.":["u17"],"copy|Primary hyperaldosteronism affects 5–10% of hypertensive patients; workup = plasma aldosterone:renin ratio, stop spironolactone 4 weeks before testing; primary hyperaldosteronism suggested by aldosterone >10 ng/dL with renin activity <1 ng/mL/hr (p1532–1533).":["u18"],"copy|Pheochromocytoma incidence <5 per million/year but becomes more common after age 50 (p1533).":["u19"],"gonadal-testes|Gonadal / Testes":["u20"],"copy|Male hypogonadism diagnosis needs symptoms/signs plus a consistently low morning testosterone on ≥2 occasions (p1533).":["u21"],"copy|Testosterone Trials (T Trials): 790 men, average age 72, baseline testosterone <275 ng/dL, treated to target 500–800 ng/dL — improved sexual function and hemoglobin/anemia, no cognitive benefit, and increased erythrocytosis and PSA/urologic referrals (p1534–1535).":["u22"],"copy|Monitor hematocrit at baseline, 3–6 months, then yearly; hematocrit >54% → reduce or stop testosterone (p1535–1536).":["u23"],"copy|PSA/DRE screening on testosterone therapy is not recommended in men ≥70 years or those with life expectancy <10 years (p1536).":["u24"],"copy|Contraindications to testosterone: active prostate or breast cancer; use caution with PSA >4.0 ng/mL, hematocrit >50, severe LUTS (IPSS >19), or recent MI/stroke (p1534).":["u25"],"copy|Reversible causes of functional hypogonadism to screen for before treating: obesity; medications (opioids, glucocorticoids, megestrol); hyperprolactinemia (usually drug-induced); malnutrition/energy deficit; chronic systemic illness (eg, malignancy); organ failure (lung, heart, liver, kidney) (Table 97-5, p1534).":["u26"],"calcium-parathyroid-vitamin-d|Calcium, Parathyroid, Vitamin D":["u27"],"copy|Primary hyperparathyroidism etiology: single adenoma 80%, diffuse hyperplasia 10–15%, multiple adenomas <5%, carcinoma <1% (p1536).":["u28"],"copy|Incidence peaks at 6.3 per 1000 person-years at age 65–74; women:men 2:1 (p1536).":["u29"],"copy|Parathyroidectomy indications: serum calcium >1 mg/dL above ULN, DXA T-score <−2.5, creatinine clearance <60 mL/min, or 24-hour urine calcium >400 mg/24h (p1526 KCP, p1538–1539).":["u30"],"copy|Hypercalcemia causes split by PTH status: PTH-dependent (common: primary hyperparathyroidism; uncommon: tertiary hyperparathyroidism, lithium, familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia, ectopic PTH) vs PTH-independent (common: malignancy — osteolytic or PTHrP-humoral — and 1,25(OH)₂-vitamin D-mediated lymphoma; uncommon: thiazides, AKI, parenteral nutrition, immobilization with malignancy/Paget, granulomatous disease, hyperthyroidism/adrenal insufficiency, vitamin D/A toxicity, milk-alkali, hyperproteinemia) (Table 97-9, p1538).":["u31"],"copy|Vitamin D sufficiency thresholds: American Geriatrics Society >30 ng/mL; Institute of Medicine >20 ng/mL; deficiency <12 ng/mL (p1526 KCP, p1539).":["u32"],"copy|Vitamin D deficiency prevalence 40–50% in community-dwelling older adults (p1539).":["u33"],"copy|Treatment: mild-moderate deficiency → vitamin D2/D3 800–1000 IU/day; severe deficiency → vitamin D3 2000 IU/day for 6–12 months, or vitamin D2 50,000 IU weekly for 8–12 weeks then vitamin D3 800–1000 IU daily (p1540).":["u34"],"copy|Toxicity is rare up to the IOM safe upper limit of 4000 IU/day; early biochemical toxicity signs appear >90 ng/mL, severe toxicity >150 ng/mL (p1540).":["u35"],"paget-disease|Paget Disease":["u36"],"copy|Prevalence 2–3% in persons 55+ in the US, predominantly White people, slightly more men (p1541).":["u37"],"copy|Distribution: pelvis ~70%, lumbar spine/proximal femur ~50%, skull ~40%, proximal tibia/humerus ~30% (p1541).":["u38"],"copy|Serum alkaline phosphatase is elevated in 90% of patients; normal/near-normal AP does not exclude monostotic disease (p1542).":["u39"],"copy|Treatment of choice: zoledronic acid 5 mg IV over 15–20 minutes, often a single dose sustaining remission 5–6 years; contraindicated if creatinine clearance <35 mL/min (p1542).":["u40"],"recall|Recall":["u41"],"copy|CHAPTER 97s / HAZZARD":["u42"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 97; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u43"],"copy|Exam traps: the chapter gives two different vitamin D deficiency thresholds in the same section — AGS <30 ng/mL vs IOM <12 ng/mL (insufficiency 12–20) (p1539) — know which guideline a question is citing.":["u44"],"copy|Also, hematocrit >50 is the caution threshold before starting testosterone (p1534) but >54 is the threshold to stop/reduce it during treatment (p1535–1536) — these are not the same number.":["u45"],"copy|3.":["u46"],"copy|What is the sick-day glucocorticoid rule for a patient with known adrenal insufficiency?":["u47"],"copy|Triple the usual daily hydrocortisone dose (about 60 mg total) for 3 days with a fever ≥101.5°F; give 100 mg IM hydrocortisone if oral intake is not possible (p1526, p1531–1532).":["u48"],"copy|4.":["u49"],"copy|What serum prolactin range defines \"mild\" hyperprolactinemia that generally does not need treatment, and what should still be checked?":["u50"],"copy|20–50 ng/mL; check a serum TSH to exclude primary hypothyroidism as the cause (p1525, p1528).":["u51"],"copy|7.":["u52"],"copy|What medication must be stopped, and for how long, before an aldosterone-to-renin ratio is drawn?":["u53"],"copy|Spironolactone, for 4 weeks (p1532–1533).":["u54"],"copy|8.":["u55"],"copy|In the Testosterone Trials, what was the baseline testosterone cutoff and the treatment target range?":["u56"],"copy|Baseline total testosterone <275 ng/dL; testosterone dosed to a target of 500–800 ng/dL (p1534–1535).":["u57"],"copy|12.":["u58"],"copy|Name two lab/imaging thresholds that would indicate parathyroidectomy in an older patient with primary hyperparathyroidism.":["u59"],"copy|Serum calcium >1 mg/dL above the upper limit of normal, and/or DXA T-score <−2.5 at any site (also creatinine clearance <60 mL/min or 24-hour urine calcium >400 mg/24h) (p1526 KCP, p1538–1539).":["u60"],"copy|14.":["u61"],"copy|What is the safe upper limit of daily vitamin D3 intake per the Institute of Medicine, and above what level does early biochemical toxicity appear?":["u62"],"copy|Safe upper limit 4000 IU/day; early biochemical indications of toxicity have been reported when 25-hydroxyvitamin D exceeded 90 ng/mL (the first indication of over-treatment is hypercalciuria) (p1540).":["u63"],"copy|15.":["u64"],"copy|What is the treatment of choice for Paget disease, and what renal function cutoff contraindicates it?":["u65"],"copy|Zoledronic acid 5 mg IV (often a single dose); contraindicated if creatinine clearance <35 mL/min (p1542).":["u66"],"copy|Answer":["u67","u68","u69","u70","u71","u72","u73"],"copy|Hyperprolactinemia tiers: mild 20–50 ng/mL (rarely needs treatment, review medicines, renal function and TSH and repeat a mild elevation after rest), moderate 50–250 ng/mL (refer — likely prolactinoma), severe >250 ng/mL (refer — almost always a large prolactinoma) (p1525, p1528).":["u8"],"copy|Primary hyperaldosteronism affects 5–10% of hypertensive patients; workup = plasma aldosterone:renin ratio, withdraw spironolactone for 4 weeks before testing when safe and feasible; primary hyperaldosteronism suggested by aldosterone >10 ng/dL with renin activity <1 ng/mL/hr (p1532–1533).":["u18"],"copy|Exam traps: the historical AGS fall-prevention goal in high-risk patients was a minimum of 30 ng/mL.":["u44"],"copy|Falling short of that goal does not itself diagnose vitamin D deficiency.":["u44"],"copy|The IOM classified values below 12 ng/mL as deficient (p1539) — know which guideline a question is citing.":["u44"],"copy|Which drug can interfere with the aldosterone-to-renin ratio, and what washout can be used when clinically safe?":["u53"],"copy|Spironolactone for 4 weeks if safe and feasible.":["u54"],"copy|Monitor potassium and blood pressure and arrange alternative therapy when needed (pages 1532–1533).":["u54"]},"maxId":73}
